## Frequently Asked Questions

### Is it normal to struggle when adjusting to college life?

Yes. College can bring changes in academic expectations, relationships, routines, responsibilities, and living arrangements. Students may need different amounts of time to adapt. If distress persists or interferes with daily functioning, consider reaching out for support.

### How long does it take to adjust to college?

There is no single timeline that applies to everyone. Some students feel comfortable within a few weeks, while others need longer to develop routines and relationships. Your adjustment may also change throughout the semester as coursework and responsibilities increase.

### What can I do if I feel lonely in college?

Start with small, manageable opportunities for connection, such as speaking with classmates, joining an interest-based group, or contacting someone you trust. Meaningful friendships often develop through repeated interactions rather than one conversation. If loneliness is persistent or affecting your well-being, counseling may help.

### How can I manage first-year college anxiety?

Identify the situations that trigger anxiety, create realistic plans for upcoming tasks, maintain basic routines, and ask for help when you need it. If anxiety is persistent, overwhelming, or interfering with classes, sleep, relationships, or everyday life, consider speaking with a qualified mental health professional.

### Does college counseling help with academic stress?

It can. Counseling may help students work through anxiety, perfectionism, fear of failure, emotional regulation, and coping strategies. For course selection, study techniques, or degree requirements, an academic advisor or campus learning center may be the more appropriate resource.

### Should I seek therapy even if I am not in a crisis?

Yes, it can be reasonable to seek therapy before a situation becomes a crisis. You may want help understanding your emotions, adjusting to independence, improving coping skills, or navigating relationships and major decisions. A therapist can help you determine whether counseling fits your needs.

